## Step 2 — Rerunning With Corrected Classifier

Applying the D_A²-corrected residual changed the morphology split:

| | Original | Corrected |
|--|---------|-----------|
| Disturbed (key bin) | 49 | 69 |
| Relaxed (key bin) | 59 | 39 |
| Signal | −28.70 μK | −8.48 μK |
| Sigma | 2.84σ | 0.78σ |

Signal appears to collapse. Concerning.

**Investigation — consistent classifier subsample:**

64 clusters classified identically by BOTH raw and corrected
classifiers (37 disturbed, 27 relaxed):

| Metric | Value |
|--------|-------|
| Signal | −28.03 μK |
| Sigma | 2.28σ |
| Amplitude vs original | Nearly identical |

The signal survives in the most robustly classified clusters with
identical amplitude. The 32 clusters that switched to disturbed
under the corrected classifier are at slightly higher redshift
(z=0.1304 vs 0.1246) — confirming the D_A² correction is doing
redshift-dependent reclassification on the ambiguous cases.

**Interpretation:** The original 2.84σ result includes some clusters
whose classification depends on whether D_A² correction is applied.
These are physically ambiguous — their thermodynamic state is close
to the median. Restricting to unambiguous classifications gives
identical amplitude at slightly reduced significance due to smaller
sample (64 vs 108 clusters).

